			<div class="content" id="post-content-1888390">
									

<p>(This critique gets a little stream-of-consciousness. Sorry for the lack of organization.)</p>
<p>I like the idea a lot, but the execution needs work. The entire dialogue part really doesn't work as such; if you read those lines out loud to yourself, they literally sound like a badly-written movie conversation. You have an uncapitalized genus (&quot;<em>mallos gregalis</em>&quot; should be &quot;<em>Mallos gregalis</em>&quot;, that's sincerely important taxonomically). The entire incident in Chile doesn't really make sense to me&#8212;what cameras are capturing this?</p>
<p>Some of the sentences can be taken out entirely, as their general length makes them sound kind of awkward; &quot;Currently it is theorised the colony is using electrosense&quot; is an example, both since a) that's a half-random hypothesis, not a theory, and b) the SCP would work just as well without it. &quot;Any mammal touching the spider web will instantly adhere and be unable to remove the web&quot; sounds a little too hokey-sci-fi; you'll want to present it more clinically, without adverbs like &quot;instantly&quot; (it would be more realistic if the spiders had <em>some</em> kind of low failure rate), and I don't see why it would necessarily be <em>mammals</em> per se. Do birds just slide off?</p>
<p>And why the redactions in the final log, the plane-crashing-into-spiders thing? The story in the log is perfect without redactions; it can literally just be &quot;plane hits spiders, spiders eat plane&quot; or &quot;plane hits spiders and blows up, spiders run away&quot; or anything else, but the redaction as is is just bothersome.</p>
<p>Anyway, I really think this could be a great SCP with fixes, but I have to neutral vote for now.</p>
<p>EDIT AGAIN: Just noticed that you use a blackboxed SCP in the containment procedures. That can go too. Just generically crosstesting SCPs with one another is a terrible idea, and blackboxing in the containment procedures is heavily frowned upon. It's a fairly easy edit, but it's probably the most glaring issue at present.</p>

							</div>

								
